L000071	\title{
L000072	
L000073	\boldmath Observation of $\Xs$ Polarization and Determination of Its Electric and Magnetic Moments in $\psi(3686)\to\XsXs$
L000074	}
L000075	\author{BESIII Collaboration}
L000076	\thanks{Full author list given at the end of the Letter.}
L000077	
L000078	
L000079	
L000080	\begin{abstract}
L000081	Using the data sample of $2.7\times10^9$ $\psi(3686)$ events collected with the BESIII detector at the BEPCII collider, we present an observation of the $\Xi(1530)^0$ polarization in the decay $\psi(3686)\to\XsXs$ with a significance larger than $20\sigma$ compared with all other tested hypotheses. The helicity amplitudes for the process $\psi(3686)\to\XsXs$ and the moduli of form factors including electric charge, magnetic dipole, electric quadrupole, and magnetic octupole are measured for the first time by performing an angular distribution analysis. Additionally, the polarization correlations between $\Xs$ and $\Xsb$ are measured.
L000082	\end{abstract}
L000083	
L000084	\maketitle
L000085	
L000086	
L000087	
L000088	
L000089	
L000090	
L000091	Currently, the widely accepted theory of the strong interaction is the quantum chromodynamics (QCD), a non-Abelian gauge field theory that describes the interactions between quarks and gluons, featuring quark asymptotic freedom and confinement. Since its inception over five decades ago, QCD has been precisely tested at high energy scales.
L000092	At low energy scales, in the exploration of baryons and their excited states, lattice QCD has played a pivotal role. By providing a nonperturbative computational framework, lattice QCD enables the direct calculation of hadron form factors, including the higher-order multipole moments that characterize high-spin particles~\cite{Nozawa:1990gt,Alexandrou:2008bn,Alexandrou:2010jv}. These multipole moments, i.e., form factors, significantly advance our understanding of baryon structure and the underlying dynamics of strong interactions.
L000093	Despite the successes obtained so far, the nonperturbative aspects of QCD at low energy scales remain poorly understood, posing a significant challenge to the field. More recently, the development of the angular distribution analysis theory within the helicity formalism, combined with the experimental conditions for baryon pair production in $\EE$ collisions, has enabled high-spin baryons to serve as new probes for precisely testing QCD~\cite{Alexandrou:2008bn,Alexandrou:2010jv}. This is supported by the direct experimental measurement of their higher-order multipole moments, which were previously inaccessible.
L000094	
L000095	Baryons, the simplest colorless systems formed by the three-color quark combination in QCD, are among the most important probes in particle and nuclear physics for understanding quark-gluon structures. However, their size~\cite{Pohl:2010zza,Carlson:2015jba}, spin~\cite{Aidala:2012mv}, and intrinsic structure~\cite{Miller:2007uy} are still not fully understood.
L000096	Since the first experimental determination of proton form factors in the 1950s~\cite{Punjabi:2015bba}, form factors have remained a fundamental property of baryons and have been extensively studied to this day.
L000097	In recent years, baryon pair production in $\EE$ collisions has provided an ideal experimental framework for extracting timelike form factors. These timelike form factors are intimately connected with more intuitive spacelike quantities, such as charge and magnetization densities, via dispersion relations~\cite{Belushkin:2006qa,Yan:2023yff}.
L000098	According to the optical theorem, the form factors in the spacelike region are real at the lowest order due to the hermiticity of the electromagnetic Hamiltonian, while in the timelike region they are complex, leading to a transverse polarization for the produced baryons~\cite{Dubnickova:1992ii,Tomasi-Gustafsson:2005svz,Denig:2012by}.
L000099	Based on this approach, BESIII has conducted extensive measurements of baryon form factors, employing angular distribution analysis~\cite{Wang:2018kdh,BESIII:2020lkm,BESIII:2021cvv,Wang:2022zyc, BESIII:2021ypr,BESIII:2022lsz,Liu:2023xhg, BESIII:2023euh,BESIII:2023ynq,BESIII:2023lkg,BESIII:2023cvk,BESIII:2024dmr,BESIII:2024nif,BESIII:2025yzk,BESIII:2025dke,BESIII:2026hgj}.
L000100	However, most of these studies have focused on spin-1/2 ground state baryons, while research on higher-spin baryons remains scarce, with only a limited number of investigations targeting the $\Omega^-$ baryon~\cite{Dobbs:2014ifa,Dobbs:2017hyd,BESIII:2020lkm,BESIII:2022kzc, Zhang:2026qjt, Zhang:2025qmo}.
L000101	
L000102	In contrast to spin-1/2 baryons, which are described by two form factors for their charge and magnetic moment, spin-3/2 baryons are characterized by four form factors, representing their electric charge($|G_E|$), magnetic dipole ($|G_M|$), electric quadrupole ($|G_Q|$), and magnetic octupole ($|G_O|$)~\cite{Korner:1976hv}, providing a broader physical insight into the baryon structure and the properties of QCD at low energy scales. Their polarization is also more complex, involving fifteen independent components~\cite{Song:1967vpt,Doncel:1972ez,Zhao:2022lbw,Zhang:2023wmd}.
L000103	A previous BESIII study on the $\Omega^-$ has observed clear multipole polarization effects~\cite{BESIII:2020lkm}, but the single-tagging method has limited the full characterization of polarization in all directions. Moreover, investigations of baryon excited states are still lacking.
L000104	As a member of the SU(3) decuplet, $\Xs$ is a well-understood cascade resonance, providing a solid foundation for experimental analysis. The angular distribution analysis based on the double-tagging method~\cite{Zhang:2023box}, combined with the multistage cascade decay of $\Xs$, further provides favorable conditions and robust constraints for clarifying the spin polarization and correlations in all directions between the baryon and the antibaryon.
L000105	
L000106	
L000107	
L000108	In this Letter, based on $(2712.4\pm14.3)\times10^6$ $\psi(3686)$ events~\cite{BESIII:2024lks} collected with the BESIII detector~\cite{BESIII:2009fln} at the BEPCII collider~\cite{Yu:2016cof}, we present the first multidimensional angular distribution analysis for the process $\EE\to\psi(3686)\to\Xs\Xsb$ and its subsequent decays. As a result, we report the observation and correlation of multipole polarization and helicity parameters of $\Xs$, along with the moduli of its form factors and their ratios for the first time.
L000109	
L000110	
L000111	
L000112	
L000113	To maximize reconstruction efficiency while minimizing background contamination, a double-tagging reconstruction technique is applied to the decays $\Xs\to\Xi^-\pi^+$, $\Xi^-\to\Lambda\pi^-$, $\Lambda\to p\pi^-$ and $\Xsb\to\bar\Xi^+\pi^-$, $\bar\Xi^+\to\bar\Lambda\pi^+$, $\bar\Lambda\to \bar{p}\pi^+$. Each signal event contains eight charged final state particles.
L000114	Monte Carlo (MC) simulated data samples produced with a {\sc geant4}-based~\cite{G42002iii} software package, which includes the geometric description of the BESIII detector and the detector response, are used to determine detection efficiencies and to estimate backgrounds. The simulation models the beam energy spread and initial state radiation (ISR) in the $e^+e^-$ annihilations with the generator {\sc kkmc}~\cite{Jadach2001}. The inclusive MC sample includes the production of the $\psi(3686)$ resonance, the ISR production of the $J/\psi$, and the continuum processes incorporated in {\sc kkmc}~\cite{Jadach2001}.
L000115	The exclusive MC samples for the signal process are generated using a phase space (PHSP) model implemented in the EvtGen event generator~\cite{Lange:2001uf,Ping:2008zz}.
L000116	
L000117	Charged tracks must be reconstructed in the main drift chamber (MDC) within the angular region $|\cos\theta|<0.93$. Here, $\theta$ is the polar angle relative to the $z$ axis in the laboratory frame, which is the symmetry axis of the MDC.
L000118	The number of positive or negative charged tracks must be at least three for each case.
L000119	Particle identification for charged tracks combines the measurements of the energy deposited in the MDC (${\rm d}E/{\rm d}x$) and the time of flight to form likelihoods $\mathcal{L}(h)$ $(h=p,K,\pi)$.
L000120	Tracks are identified as protons when the proton hypothesis has the greatest likelihood $[\mathcal{L}(p)>\mathcal{L}(K)~{\rm and}~\mathcal{L}(p)>\mathcal{L}(\pi)]$, while charged pions are identified by requiring $[\mathcal{L}(\pi)>\mathcal{L}(K)~{\rm and}~\mathcal{L}(\pi)>\mathcal{L}(p)]$.
L000121	Events with one proton, one antiproton, at least three pions of the same charge, and at least two pions of the opposite charge are selected for the analysis.
L000122	For the pion type ($\pi^+$ or $ \pi^-$) with only two candidates, one additional missing pion of the same charge is assumed in the reconstruction.
L000123	In cases where both the numbers of $\pi^+$ and $\pi^-$ candidates exceed two, the reconstruction procedure is performed by treating either one $\pi^+$ or one $\pi^-$ candidate as a missing particle, the motivation for which will be clarified later.
L000124	The combination yielding the smallest total $\chi^2$, defined as the sum of the contributions from the secondary vertex fit and kinematic fit described below, is selected.
L000125	Given the identical reconstruction logic for both cases, the following description focuses solely on the missing $\pi^-$ case.
L000126	
L000127	To reconstruct $\Lambda(\bar\Lambda)$, $\Xi^-(\bar\Xi^+)$, and $\Xs$ candidates, all combinations of final state particle candidates are created, and vertex fit and secondary vertex fit~\cite{Xu:2009zzg} are applied sequentially.
L000128	The combination with the minimum quadratic sum of the residuals between the reconstructed invariant masses of all intermediate states and their corresponding known values~\cite{ParticleDataGroup:2024cfk} is selected.
L000129	Additionally, the $\Lambda(\bar\Lambda)$ and $\Xi^-(\bar\Xi^+)$ decay lengths  are required to be greater than zero to suppress backgrounds from non-$\Lambda(\bar\Lambda)$ and non-$\Xi^-(\bar\Xi^+)$ events, where negative values of decay length can result from detector resolution.
L000130	To further suppress combinatorial backgrounds and improve detector resolution, a one-constraint ($1C$) kinematic fit is employed. The fit ensures energy–momentum conservation with four constraints, while the missing $\pi^-$ track from $\Xsb$ is treated as 3 additional degrees of freedom, and events are required to satisfy $\chi^2_{1C} < 40$.
L000131	Note that even when sufficient $\pi^-$ candidates are available, full reconstruction does not reduce the background level because the backgrounds have the same final state as discussed below. Therefore, it is also treated using the missing particle approach to improve efficiency.
L000132	To eliminate the primary background from the $\EE\to\pi^+\pi^-J/\psi\to\pi^+\pi^-\Xi^-\bar\Xi^+$ process, a veto requirement of $M^{\rm Recoil}_{\pi^+\pi^-}\notin[3.077,~3.117]$ GeV/$c^2$ is applied, where $M^{\rm Recoil}_{\pi^+\pi^-}$ is the recoil mass of the $\pi^+\pi^-$ combination.
L000133	After applying the above selection criteria, the invariant masses of the $p\pi^-(\bar{p}\pi^+)$, $\Lambda\pi^-(\bar\Lambda\pi^+)$, and $\Xi^-\pi^+(\bar\Xi^+\pi^-)$ combinations are required within the ranges [1.111, 1.121], [1.314, 1.330], and [1.490, 1.588] GeV/$c^2$, respectively.
L000134	Figure~\ref{2Dbox} shows the two-dimensional distribution of $M_{\bar\Xi^+\pi^-}$ versus $M_{\Xi^-\pi^+}$, where $M_{\bar\Xi^+\pi^-}$ and $M_{\Xi^-\pi^+}$ represent the invariant masses of the $\bar\Xi^+\pi^-$ and $\Xi^-\pi^+$ pairs, respectively. A clear enhancement within the mass requirement for the $\Xi^-\pi^+(\bar\Xi^+\pi^-)$ combination, marked by the red box, is observed.
L000135	
L000136	\begin{figure}[!htbp]
L000137	    \begin{center}
L000138	    \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{box_data.pdf}
L000139	    \end{center}
L000140	    \vspace{-0.5cm}
L000141	    \caption{Distribution of $M_{\bar\Xi^+\pi^-}$ versus $M_{\Xi^-\pi^+}$. The solid box marked by S shows the signal region, and the dashed boxes marked by $B_1$ and $B_2$ denote the sideband regions.}
L000142	    \label{2Dbox}
L000143	\end{figure}
L000144	
L000145	An inclusive MC sample of $\psi(3686)$, matching the size of the data, is used to study the background sources. The primary background in this analysis arises from the two three-body processes, $\psi(3686)\to \bar\Xi^+\pi^-\Xi(1530)^0$ and $\psi(3686)\to \Xi^-\pi^+\bar\Xi(1530)^0$, which exhibit peaks in the distribution of $M_{\Xi^-\pi^+}$ and $M_{\bar\Xi^+\pi^-}$ on the side associated with the excited $\Xi$ states.
L000146	The impact of other nonpeaking backgrounds, mainly $\psi(3686)\to\pi^+\pi^-J/\psi$ and $\psi(3686)\to\pi^+\pi^-\Xi^-\bar\Xi^+$, is evaluated via MC simulations, yielding 2.2\% and 0.9\%, respectively.
L000147	The contribution of peaking backgrounds is estimated from sideband regions, chosen with reference to the distributions from exclusive MC simulations, and defined as
L000148	$B_1$, $M_{\Xi^-\pi^+} \in [1.490,~1.588]$~GeV/$c^2$ $\cap$ $M_{\bar\Xi^+\pi^-} \in [1.890,~1.988]$~GeV/$c^2$, and $B_2$, $M_{\Xi^-\pi^+} \in [1.884,~1.982]$~GeV/$c^2$ $\cap$ $M_{\bar\Xi^+\pi^-} \in [1.490,~1.588]$~GeV/$c^2$, each with the same size as the signal region. These regions are marked with the dashed boxes in Fig.~\ref{2Dbox}.
L000149	Based on the sideband method, the signal yield is extracted as $N_{\rm sig} = N_{\rm obs} - N_{\rm bkg} = 1894\pm53$, where $N_{\rm obs}$ is the number of events in the signal region, and $N_{\rm bkg}=451\pm21$ represents the estimated background yield calculated as $N_{B_1} + N_{B_2}$. Here, $N_{B_1}$ and $N_{B_2}$ denote the numbers of events in the sideband regions, respectively.
L000150	
L000151	
L000152	
L000153	
L000154	The joint angular distribution describes the spin correlation and polarization of $\Xs$ and $\Xsb$, which is expressed as~\cite{Jacob:1959at,Perotti:2018wxm,BESIII:2020lkm,Zhang:2023box}
L000155	\begin{align}
L000156	    \mathscr{W}(\bm{\xi}; \bm{\Omega}) ={}& \sum_{\mu=0}^{15}\sum_{\nu=0}^{15}\sum_{m=0}^{3}\sum_{n=0}^{3}\sum_{i=0}^{3}\sum_{j=0}^{3} S_{\mu\nu} b_{\mu m}^{\Xi(1530)^0} b_{\nu n}^{\bar\Xi(1530)^0} \notag \\
L000157	    & \times a_{mi}^{\Xi^-} a_{nj}^{\bar\Xi^+} a_{i0}^{\Lambda} a_{j0}^{\bar\Lambda}.
L000158	    \label{cs}
L000159	\end{align}
L000160	Here, the matrix $S$ represents the spin density matrix, which is formulated in terms of four helicity amplitudes~\cite{Perotti:2018wxm},
L000161	while $b$ and $a$ are the polarization transfer matrices for spin-3/2 and spin-1/2 particles, respectively. The vector $\bm{\xi}$ represents thirteen helicity angles, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{xyz}, and $\bm{\Omega}$ denotes the helicity and decay parameters, with two distinct parametrization schemes for the helicity parameters.
L000162	For the first parametrization, the parameters $h_i$ and $\phi'_i$ ($i=1,3,4$)~\cite{BESIII:2020lkm} define the ratios of the helicity amplitudes and their relative phase differences, while in the second parametrization the helicity amplitudes are expressed in terms of the angular distribution parameters $\alpha_{\psi}$, $\alpha_1$, and $\alpha_2$, with $\phi_i$ ($i=1,3,4$)~\cite{Zhang:2023box} denoting the relative phase differences. Since the second parametrization corresponds to that for spin-1/2 particles, it provides a simpler connection to the polarization observables. This analysis is primarily based on the second parametrization, while the results obtained with the first parametrization are also provided. The correspondence between the two parametrizations is detailed in Ref.~\cite{Zhang:2023box}. Additionally, the weak decay parameters $\alpha_{\Xi^-}$, $\alpha_{\bar{\Xi}^+}$, $\phi_{\Xi^-}$, $\phi_{\bar{\Xi}^+}$, $\alpha_{\Lambda}$, and $\alpha_{\bar{\Lambda}}$ are well defined in Ref.~\cite{Perotti:2018wxm}.
L000163	
L000164	
L000165	
L000166	\begin{figure}[!hbpt]
L000167		\begin{center}
L000168	        \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{Xi1530_xyz.pdf}
L000169		\end{center}
L000170	        \vspace{-0.5cm}
L000171		\caption{The definitions of the helicity angles. The $\Xi^{*0}$ and $\bar\Xi^{*0}$ denote $\Xs$ and $\Xsb$, respectively. The polar angle $\theta_{\Xi^{*0}}$ is the angle between the $\Xs$ momentum and the $e^+$ beam direction in the $\EE$ center-of-mass frame, where the $\hat{z}_{e^+}$ axis is defined along the $e^+$ momentum. $\theta_{\Xi^-}$ and $\phi_{\Xi^-}$ are the polar and azimuthal angles of the $\Xi^-$ momentum direction in the $\Xs$ rest frame, where $\hat{z}_{\Xi^{*0}}$ is defined along the $\Xs$ momentum direction in the $\EE$ center-of-mass frame, and $\hat{y}_{\Xi^{*0}}$ is defined by $\hat{z}\times\hat{z}_{\Xi^{*0}}$. The definitions of $\theta_{\Lambda}$, $\phi_{\Lambda}$, $\theta_{p}$, and $\phi_{p}$ follow the same convention as above.}
L000172		\label{xyz}
L000173	\end{figure}
L000174	
L000175	\begin{figure*}[hbpt]
L000176	    \begin{center}
L000177	    \includegraphics[width=0.32\textwidth]{pol_1530_w.pdf}
L000178	    \includegraphics[width=0.32\textwidth]{pol_corr_small.pdf}
L000179	    \includegraphics[width=0.32\textwidth]{pol_corr_big.pdf}
L000180	    \end{center}
L000181	    \vspace{-0.5cm}
L000182	    \caption{The $\cos\theta_{\Xs}$ dependence of the nonzero independent polarization components (left) and the polarization correlation terms (middle and right). Here, $S_{i,i}$ ($i=0,1,2,...,14$) denotes the diagonal elements of the $S$ matrix, with $S_{0,0}$ representing the cross section term used for normalization. The solid lines represent the central values, and the shaded areas represent the ranges of 1 standard deviation.}
L000183	    \label{pol}
L000184	\end{figure*}
L000185	
L000186	An unbinned maximum likelihood fit is performed to extract the set of $\Xs$ polarization parameters $\bm{\Omega}$. In the fit, the weak decay parameters for both $\Xi^-(\bar\Xi^+)$ and $\Lambda(\bar\Lambda)$ are fixed according to the current highest-precision measurements~\cite{BESIII:2021ypr,BESIII:2022qax}, assuming charge-parity conservation. The likelihood function $\mathscr{L}$ is constructed based on the probability density function, $\mathscr{P}(\bm{\xi}_i)$, for the event $i$ characterized by the measured angles $\bm{\xi}_i$
L000187	\begin{equation}
L000188	    \mathscr{L}=\prod_{i=1}^{N}\mathscr{P}(\bm{\xi}_i;\bm{\Omega})=\prod_{i=1}^{N}\mathcal{C}\mathscr{W}(\bm{\xi}_i;\bm{\Omega})\epsilon(\bm{\xi}_i),
L000189	\end{equation}
L000190	where $N$ is the number of events in the signal region, the joint angular distribution $\mathscr{W}(\bm{\xi}_i;\bm{\Omega})$ is given in Eq.~(\ref{cs}), and $\epsilon(\bm{\xi}_i)$ is the detection efficiency.
L000191	The normalization factor $\mathcal{C}^{-1}=\frac{1}{N_{\rm MC}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\rm MC}}\mathscr{W}(\bm{\xi}_j;\bm{\Omega})$ is calculated as the sum of the corresponding amplitudes $\mathscr{W}$ from the accepted PHSP MC events $N_{\rm MC}$, applying the same event selection criteria as for the data.
L000192	The minimization of the objective function defined as $\mathcal{S} = -\ln\mathscr{L}_{\rm S} + \ln\mathscr{L}_{\rm B}$ is conducted using the MINUIT package from the ROOT library~\cite{James:1975dr}.
L000193	Here, $\mathscr{L}_{\rm S}$ and $\mathscr{L}_{\rm B}$ represent the likelihood functions for events chosen in the signal and sideband regions, respectively.
L000194	Since the signal and sideband regions cover different mass ranges and thus have different efficiencies, we apply a multidimensional, machine-learning-based correction to the sideband data to match the signal region efficiency, with correction factors derived from MC simulations.
L000195	
L000196	The numerical results obtained from the maximum likelihood fit to the data, along with the moduli of the form factors and their ratios calculated based on the relationships provided in Ref.~\cite{Zhang:2023box}, are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:solution}.
L000197	The first row and the first column of the matrix $S$ in Eq.~(\ref{cs}) represent the spin polarization components of $\Xs$ and $\Xsb$, respectively, while its diagonal terms denote the polarization correlation coefficients between $\Xs$ and $\Xsb$. Among these, the nonzero independent terms, calculated based on the measured parameters, include the spin vector ($S_T^y$), rank-2 spin tensor ($S_{LL}$, $S_{LT}^x$, $S_{TT}^{xx}$), and rank-3 spin tensor ($S_{LLT}^y$, $S_{LTT}^{xy}$)~\cite{Zhang:2023wmd,Zhang:2023box} for the polarization components, as well as the independent terms for the correlation coefficients, all of which are displayed in Fig.~\ref{pol}.
L000198	To illustrate the model’s description of the data, we define the moments
L000199	\begin{align}
L000200	    M_{\mu\nu}(\cos\theta_{\Xi(1530)^0}) =  &\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=0}^{N}\sum_{i=0}^{3}\sum_{j=0}^{3}\sum_{k=0}^{3}\sum_{l=0}^{3}b^{\Xi(1530)^0}_{\mu i}b^{\bar\Xi(1530)^0}_{\nu j} \\ \notag
L000201	    &\times a^{\Xi^-}_{ik}a^{\bar\Xi^+}_{jl}a^{\Lambda}_{k0}a^{\bar\Lambda}_{l0}
L000202	\end{align}
L000203	for projecting six polarization terms of the $\Xi(1530)^0$, as shown in Fig.~\ref{mom}. Here, $N$ denotes the number of signal events in the data, and $\mu\nu$ represents the corresponding element of the matrix $S$ in Eq.~(\ref{cs}).
L000204	For each polarization component, the polarization degree is evaluated by integrating its absolute value over $\theta_{\Xs}$, and the significance is determined by the ratio of the central value to its uncertainty. The significances of $S_T^y$, $S_{LL}$, $S_{LT}^x$, $S_{TT}^{xx}$, $S_{LLT}^{y}$, and $S_{LTT}^{xy}$, after taking into account systematic uncertainties, are $3.3\sigma$, $16.9\sigma$, $2.6\sigma$, $8.5\sigma$, $5.6\sigma$, and $8.1\sigma$, respectively. The total significance, obtained by weighting all components~\cite{Zhang:2023box}, is $20.9\sigma$.
L000205	
L000206	\begin{figure}[!hbpt]
L000207	    \begin{center}
L000208	    \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{comb_moment.pdf}
L000209	    \end{center}
L000210	    \vspace{-0.5cm}
L000211	    \caption{Comparison of $M_{\mu\nu}$ distributions between data and MC. The blue histograms represent the PHSP MC samples, while the orange histograms represent the MC weighted by the measured parameters. The black dots with error bars are data.}
L000212	    \label{mom}
L000213	\end{figure}
L000214	
L000215	\begin{table}[!htbp]
L000216	    \caption{Numerical results and uncertainties of the helicity parameters, the moduli of form factors, and their ratios. The first and second uncertainties are statistical and systematic, respectively.}
L000217	    \centering
L000218	    \scalebox{0.88}{
L000219	    \begin{tabular}{c c  c r}
L000220	    \hline
L000221	    \hline
L000222	    Parameter        &\multicolumn{1}{c}{Value}  &Parameter        &\multicolumn{1}{c}{Value}\\
L000223	    \hline
L000224	    $\alpha_{\psi}$  &$~~0.02\pm0.06\pm0.06$      &$h_1$            &$ 0.69\pm0.05\pm0.05$ \\
L000225	    $\alpha_1$       &$-0.42\pm0.08\pm0.05$       &$h_3$            &$ 0.43\pm0.04\pm0.04$ \\
L000226	    $\alpha_2$       &$~~0.75\pm0.06\pm0.06$      &$h_4$            &$ 0.43\pm0.03\pm0.03$ \\
L000227	    $\phi_1$ (rad)   &$-1.81\pm0.13\pm0.07$       &$\phi_1'$ (rad)  &$ 0.56\pm0.11\pm0.06$ \\
L000228	    $\phi_3$ (rad)   &$~~2.37\pm0.08\pm0.05$      &$\phi_3'$ (rad)  &$ 2.37\pm0.08\pm0.05$ \\
L000229	    $\phi_4$ (rad)   &$~~1.02\pm0.15\pm0.08$      &$\phi_4'$ (rad)  &$-2.90\pm0.13\pm0.08$ \\[1ex]
